## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design practical backpressure and degradation strategies for a CNPA stack under load, balancing user impact with data integrity.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and load shedding
- Degraded paths and asynchronous processing
- Idempotency and deduplication across HTTP, DB, and Kafka
- Observability: latency, error rate, queue depth, saturation signals

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst; // max tokens
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  consume(n = 1) {
    this._refresh();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
  _refresh() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement end-to-end idempotency to prevent duplicates during backpressure? 
- What metrics and rollback criteria would you rely on to trigger a full rollback or canary pause?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes your ability to diagnose multi-service latency issues under multi-tenant load, with realistic trade-offs between per-tenant isolation, backpressure, and end-to-end tracing.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing and context propagation across HTTP, Redis, DB, Kafka, and sink
- Per-tenant rate budgets and backpressure
- Tail latency analysis and query plan instrumentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: OpenTelemetry span around HTTP handler and downstream calls
const span = tracer.startSpan("http.request", { parent: ctx });
try {
  // propagate span context to Redis and Kafka clients
  await redisClient.call("LIMIT", { tenantId: tenant }, { span });
  await kafkaProducer.send({ key: tenant, value: payload, span });
} finally {
  span.end();
}
```

